<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-span" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Span of a Set of Vectors</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Several figures in this section and the next are interactive: the violet region is
      the span, and moving the sliders (click <q>Open Controls</q>) forms the linear combination
      shown in the equation. They are powered by the demo framework from
      <pubtitle>Interactive Linear Algebra</pubtitle> by Margalit and Rabinoff
      (<url href="https://github.com/QBobWatson/ila" visual="github.com/QBobWatson/ila"/>),
      used under the GPL.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <definition xml:id="def-span">
    <title>Span</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \vec{u}_2, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}</m> be a set of vectors in
        <m>\R^n</m>. Then the collection of all linear combinations of these vectors is
        called the <term>span</term> of these vectors, written
        <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1, \vec{u}_2, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="eg-span-plane">
    <title>Describing a Span</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Describe the span of the vectors
        <m>\vec{u} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
        <m>\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Note that any linear combination of <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> is of the
        form
        <md>
          a_1 \vec{u} + a_2 \vec{v}
          = a_1 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          + a_2 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ a_1 + 2a_2 \\ 2a_1 + 3a_2 \end{bmatrix},
        </md>
        which can be thought of as a vector in the <m>yz</m>-plane.
      </p>
      <p>
        Also, consider an arbitrary vector
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}</m> in the <m>yz</m>-plane. We can
        write any such vector as a linear combination of <m>\vec{u}</m> and
        <m>\vec{v}</m>. Solving
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
          = x_1 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          + x_2 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
        </md>
        amounts to row reducing
        <md>
          \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp 2 \amp y \\ 2 \amp 3 \amp z \end{array} \right]
          \longrightarrow
          \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp 2 \amp y \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp z - 2y \end{array} \right]
          \longrightarrow
          \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp 0 \amp -3y + 2z \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2y - z \end{array} \right],
        </md>
        giving <m>x_1 = -3y + 2z</m> and <m>x_2 = 2y - z</m>. That is,
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
          = (-3y + 2z) \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          + (2y - z) \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Hence <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}, \vec{v} \}</m> is the <m>yz</m>-plane.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="eg-span-adding-vector">
    <title>What Happens When We Add a Third Vector?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the previous example, where the span of <m>\vec{u}</m> and
        <m>\vec{v}</m> was the <m>yz</m>-plane. Suppose we add another vector
        <m>\vec{w}</m>, and consider the span of <m>\vec{u}</m>, <m>\vec{v}</m>, and
        <m>\vec{w}</m>. What would happen to the span?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <term>Case (I): <m>\vec{w}</m> is also a vector in the <m>yz</m>-plane.</term>
        For example, consider <m>\vec{w} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        Then <m>\vec{w}</m> is already in <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}, \vec{v} \}</m>:
        <md>
          \vec{w} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}
          = -5\vec{u} + 3\vec{v}
          = -5 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          + 3 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
        Hence
        <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}, \vec{v}, \vec{w} \} = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}, \vec{v} \}</m>
        <mdash /> adding <m>\vec{w}</m> gained us nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Case (II): <m>\vec{w}</m> is not in the <m>yz</m>-plane.</term>
        For example, <m>\vec{w} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Notice
        that any vector in <m>\R^3</m> can be written as a linear combination of
        <m>\vec{u}</m>, <m>\vec{v}</m>, and <m>\vec{w}</m>:
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
          = x_1 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          + x_2 \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
          + x_3 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
        </md>
        where
        <md>
          x_1 = 2z - 3y - 3x, \qquad x_2 = 2x + 2y - z, \qquad x_3 = x.
        </md>
        Hence <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}, \vec{v}, \vec{w} \} = \R^3</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The moral: a new vector enlarges the span exactly when it is not already in the
        span. This is the idea that the next section makes precise.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-seeing-a-span">
    <title>Seeing a Span</title>

    <p>
      The examples above were settled by row reduction. The same facts can be seen
      directly: the span of two vectors in <m>\R^3</m> that are not multiples of one
      another is a plane through the origin, and a vector lies in that span exactly when
      it lies on that plane.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-span-plane-hit">
      <caption>
        The span of <m>\vec{v}_1 = (1,1,0)</m> and <m>\vec{v}_2 = (0,1,2)</m> is the
        violet plane. Move the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> sliders (click <q>Open Controls</q>) until
        the red vector <m>x\vec{v}_1 + y\vec{v}_2</m> reaches the target
        <m>\vec{w} = (2,3,2)</m>. It can be done, because <m>\vec{w}</m> lies on the
        plane: the values <m>x = 2</m> and <m>y = 1</m> turn the <m>\neq</m> in the
        equation into an <m>=</m>.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="int-span-plane-hit" iframe="demos/spans.html?v1=1,1,0&amp;v2=0,1,2&amp;target=2,3,2&amp;range=4&amp;camera=2.4,.7,1.6&amp;closed=true" width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-span-plane-miss">
      <caption>
        The same two vectors, but now the target is <m>\vec{w} = (1,1,1)</m>. Move the
        sliders as much as you like: the <m>\neq</m> never becomes an <m>=</m>, because
        <m>\vec{w}</m> sits off the plane. So
        <m>\operatorname{Span}\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2\}</m> is a plane, not all of
        <m>\R^3</m>.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="int-span-plane-miss" iframe="demos/spans.html?v1=1,1,0&amp;v2=0,1,2&amp;target=1,1,1&amp;range=4&amp;camera=2.4,.7,1.6&amp;closed=true" width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <remark xml:id="rem-span-normal">
      <p>
        The plane in these two figures has a compact description. A vector is
        perpendicular to both <m>\vec{v}_1</m> and <m>\vec{v}_2</m> exactly when it is
        perpendicular to the whole plane, and
        <m>\vec{n} = (2,-2,1)</m> is such a vector. So
        <md>
          \operatorname{Span}\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2\}
          = \left\{ (x,y,z) \in \R^3 \; : \; 2x - 2y + z = 0 \right\}.
        </md>
        Substituting confirms the two figures: <m>\vec{w} = (2,3,2)</m> gives
        <m>4 - 6 + 2 = 0</m>, while <m>\vec{w} = (1,1,1)</m> gives
        <m>2 - 2 + 1 = 1 \neq 0</m>.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-span-challenge">
    <title>A Harder Example</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-span-parameter">
      <title>When Does a Third Vector Fail to Enlarge the Span?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <md>
            \vec{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
            \vec{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \qquad
            \vec{v}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ h \\ -4 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          where <m>h</m> is a real number.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                For which values of <m>h</m> is
                <m>\operatorname{Span}\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_3\} = \R^3</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                For the exceptional value of <m>h</m>, describe the span by an equation
                and write <m>\vec{v}_3</m> explicitly in terms of <m>\vec{v}_1</m> and
                <m>\vec{v}_2</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(1)</term> The three vectors span <m>\R^3</m> exactly when
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is consistent for every <m>\vec{b}</m>, where
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} \vec{v}_1 \amp \vec{v}_2 \amp \vec{v}_3 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          By <xref ref="thm-invertible-matrix-theorem" text="type-global" />, for a square
          matrix that happens exactly when <m>A</m> is invertible, i.e., when
          <m>\det A \neq 0</m>. Expanding along the first row,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \det A \amp = \begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp h \\ -1 \amp 1 \amp -4 \end{vmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = 1(-4 - h) - 2(-8 + h) + 1(2 + 1)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = -4 - h + 16 - 2h + 3 = 15 - 3h.
            </mrow>
          </md>
          So <m>\det A = 0</m> precisely when <m>h = 5</m>, and therefore
          <md>
            \operatorname{Span}\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_3\} = \R^3
            \qquad \text{for every } h \neq 5.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(2)</term> Take <m>h = 5</m>, so
          <m>\vec{v}_3 = (1,5,-4)</m>. Solving
          <m>a\vec{v}_1 + b\vec{v}_2 = \vec{v}_3</m> gives
          <m>a + 2b = 1</m> and <m>2a + b = 5</m>, hence <m>a = 3</m> and
          <m>b = -1</m>; the third coordinate checks out, since
          <m>-3 - 1 = -4</m>. So
          <md>
            \vec{v}_3 = 3\vec{v}_1 - \vec{v}_2,
          </md>
          which means <m>\vec{v}_3</m> adds nothing:
          <m>\operatorname{Span}\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_3\}
          = \operatorname{Span}\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2\}</m>, a plane.
        </p>
        <p>
          A vector perpendicular to both <m>\vec{v}_1</m> and <m>\vec{v}_2</m> is
          <m>\vec{n} = (1,-1,-1)</m>, since
          <m>\vec{n} \cdot \vec{v}_1 = 1 - 2 + 1 = 0</m> and
          <m>\vec{n} \cdot \vec{v}_2 = 2 - 1 - 1 = 0</m>. Hence, when <m>h = 5</m>,
          <md>
            \operatorname{Span}\{\vec{v}_1, \vec{v}_2, \vec{v}_3\}
            = \left\{ (x,y,z) \in \R^3 \; : \; x - y - z = 0 \right\},
          </md>
          and indeed <m>\vec{v}_3 = (1,5,-4)</m> satisfies <m>1 - 5 + 4 = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-span-three-coplanar">
      <caption>
        The exceptional case <m>h = 5</m> of
        <xref ref="eg-span-parameter" text="type-global" />. All three vectors lie on the
        plane <m>x - y - z = 0</m>, so the span is that plane rather than <m>\R^3</m>.
        There are now three sliders, but the red vector
        <m>x\vec{v}_1 + y\vec{v}_2 + z\vec{v}_3</m> can never leave the violet plane.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="int-span-three-coplanar" iframe="demos/spans.html?v1=1,2,-1&amp;v2=2,1,1&amp;v3=1,5,-4&amp;target=4,3,1&amp;range=8&amp;camera=1.9,.85,1.3&amp;closed=true" width="100%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>
  </subsection>
</section>
